  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="failure" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 04</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Making failure a normal state</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              When the system can't understand what was said, that attempt is
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">not thrown away</strong>. It gets
              saved, flagged with why it failed (no child's name could be matched, or
              the type of action couldn't be worked out), along with the raw words
              spoken, a translation, and the original audio recording. A badge in the
              sidebar shows how many are waiting for review.
            </p>
            <p>
              On the review screen the failed utterance renders as
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">clickable word chips</strong>. Tapping
              the word that <em>was</em> the child&rsquo;s name appends it to that
              child&rsquo;s stored phonetic alias list, and the same mishearing then routes
              correctly forever. Repair work compounds instead of repeating: a single
              correction permanently improves recognition for that name, well beyond the
              one record it fixed.
            </p>
            <p>
              Two supporting decisions matter as much. Partial understanding is shown rather
              than discarded, so the provider fills one gap instead of re-entering the
              event, and the spoken time carries through untouched rather than being replaced
              with the moment of correction. And spoken confirmation hedges rather than
              errors: a failed parse says <em>&ldquo;processing unknown event for
              unrecognized child&rdquo;</em> rather than announcing a failure. What the
              provider hears is that something was captured and needs attention, which is
              true, without an error sound in a room full of children.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <Plate
        index={screen('04-logged-events').index}
        title={screen('04-logged-events').title}
        caption={screen('04-logged-events').note}
