    <Container width="content" class="mt-16">
      <figure class="case-layout-cont border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
        <blockquote class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight text-ink leading-snug">
          We couldn&rsquo;t spot the child&rsquo;s name in this event. Help us by
          selecting a word below that sounds like the name. If there&rsquo;s no match,
          no worries, just ignore this step!
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="mono-label mt-5">The repair screen, verbatim</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="pipeline" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 05</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">The voice pipeline, and where the model goes</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              This is where design and engineering stopped being separable, and the reason
              the role was product design engineering rather than either half. A custom
              wake-word detector runs directly on the device, inside the browser, always
              listening for &ldquo;log a tot,&rdquo; so
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">no audio leaves the device just to
              detect the wake word</strong>. In a product whose microphone is live in a
              room full of other people&rsquo;s children, that is not a performance
              detail. It is the difference between an assistant and a surveillance
              device, and it is the answer to the first question every provider asked.
            </p>
            <p>
              Turning speech into a saved record
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">runs on a fixed set of rules, and
              there's no AI language model anywhere in that loop</strong>. What was said gets checked against an
              ordered list of 22 known phrasings, and child names are matched against the
              class roster plus each child&rsquo;s list of likely mishearings, so one
              sentence can log an event for several children at once. An AI model sitting
              in that spot would be slower, less predictable, and worst of all
              confidently wrong: a guessed category could write a nap that never happened
              into a record a parent reads and a state inspector may check. The
              rule-based system either finds a match or it doesn&rsquo;t, and anything it
              can&rsquo;t match goes to a queue for a person to review, with the audio
              attached. A non-match is treated as a normal, expected outcome, not a bug.
            </p>
            <p>
              The AI model gets used <em>ahead</em> of the problem instead. When a child
              is added, an automated task asks an AI model for every likely variation,
              pronunciation, and mishearing of that name, and saves them as a list the
              matching system can check against. Toddler names are short, similar to each
              other, and often not English, so recognizing them correctly is the
              product&rsquo;s single biggest challenge. The system predicts ahead of time
              how a name is likely to be misheard and teaches itself to accept those
              versions too, catching the mistake before it ever happens. It is the direct
              answer to the accent-related transcription problem the provider calls
              surfaced. Corrections a person makes in the repair queue get added to that
              same list, so the system keeps improving from both directions.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rest is shape and speed. Spanish speech gets translated to English
              <em>before</em> the system tries to match it, rather than needing a whole
              separate set of matching rules for Spanish, so one set of phrasings covers
              both languages instead of two that could drift apart. If someone mentions
              one child, the screen updates right away. If they mention several children
              at once, that takes more processing and happens a moment later in the
              background, and the audio recording uploads separately so it
              doesn&rsquo;t slow down the immediate response. The row on screen then
              fills in live, in the spot that was already waiting for it, once
              processing finishes.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

