  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" class="border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Alongside</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Accessibility, running in both directions</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              Voice is itself an accessibility feature: hands-free capture serves a provider
              holding a child the same way it serves anyone who cannot comfortably use a
              keyboard mid-task. But a voice product must not be voice-only, so every event
              capturable by speech is capturable by hand, and the repair queue means a failed
              recognition always has a manual path to resolution.
            </p>
            <p>
              On the interface side, the decisions that held up best were structural. Every
              button in the application inherits its keyboard-focus outline from one
              shared base style, so it gets a visible outline
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">by construction rather than by
              remembering</strong> to add one each time. A pop-up dialog returns keyboard
              focus, when it closes, to whatever was focused before it opened. Voice
              status is read aloud to a screen reader the instant it changes, so the same
              information a sighted user reads from a pulsing dot reaches a
              screen-reader user too, and that pulsing animation turns off automatically
              for anyone who has asked their device to reduce motion. The calendar tells
              event types apart with solid, dashed, and dotted bars rather than color,
              which still works on a month sheet printed in black and white and taped to
              the fridge. And printed reports always come out in plain black text on
              white, even if the provider's screen is set to dark mode, which is exactly
              the kind of bug that never turns up in a design review.
            </p>
            <p>
              Translation goes deeper than swapping out button labels, because Yari's
              interview said so directly: <em>&ldquo;A translated button is not enough.
              We need the instructions, warnings, support, and official documents to be
              clear too.&rdquo;</em> English and Spanish sit at parity, roughly 1,220
              lines each, covering marketing copy, the app itself, dropdown options, and
              error messages, and the system decides which language to show by
              checking, in order, what the user has explicitly chosen, what the web
              address specifies, what they picked last time, and finally what language
              their browser is set to. Date formatting changes based on language, spoken
              announcements translate for whoever is listening, and Spanish speech gets
              translated before the voice system tries to match it. The failure path is
              the one most products skip, and the one Yari named as the whole point.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

